<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">U Nu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Thakin Nu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">U Nu (1907–1995) was a Burmese nationalist and statesman who became the first prime minister of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in 1948. A leader of the Thakin movement and foreign minister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ba-maw">Ba Maw</a>'s wartime government, he succeeded the murdered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a> as head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-fascist-peoples-freedom-league">Anti-Fascist People's Freedom League</a>. He governed, with interruptions, until General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ne-win">Ne Win</a>'s military coup of March 1962 overthrew him, and he later led an armed resistance from exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/u-nu.jpg" alt="U Nu" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anefo; derivative work by Supakit prem · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Staatsgreep_in_Birma,_premier_U_nu,_Bestanddeelnr_913-5820_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nu was born on 25 May 1907 to U San Tun and Daw Saw Khin of Wakema in British Burma. He took a B.A. at Rangoon University in 1929 and became president of the Rangoon University Students Union, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a> as editor of its magazine. Both were expelled after an article in the union magazine, and their expulsion sparked the second university students' strike in February 1936. They joined the nationalist Dobama Asiayone and took the prefix Thakin, meaning master, to proclaim that they were the true masters of their own land.</p><p>The colonial government detained him in 1940. From August 1943 he was foreign minister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ba-maw">Ba Maw</a>'s regime, under the nominal independence the Japanese declared, and in 1944 minister of information. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a> he did not join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-fascist-peoples-freedom-league">AFPFL</a>'s rebellion of March 1945. After Aung San and his ministers were assassinated on 19 July 1947, Nu led the AFPFL and in October 1947 signed an independence agreement with the British prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-attlee">Clement Attlee</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> became independent on 4 January 1948, and Nu became its first prime minister. He faced armed rebellion from ethnic groups, two communist factions and some army regiments, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> forces who had set up bases in eastern Burma and took several years in the early 1950s to drive out. He oversaw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyidawtha">Pyidawtha Plan</a> for economic development. A devout Theravada Buddhist, he had the Kaba Aye Pagoda and the Maha Pasana Guha built in 1952 for the Sixth Buddhist Synod, which he hosted in 1954 to 1956.</p><p>On 26 September 1958 he asked General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ne-win">Ne Win</a> to take over as a caretaker government. His Clean faction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-fascist-peoples-freedom-league">AFPFL</a> won the election of February 1960 and he formed a government on 4 April 1960. In 1961 an act of Parliament made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> the state religion, which alienated Christian minorities such as the Kachins and became one of the main factors in the Kachin conflict. Ne Win overthrew him in a coup on 2 March 1962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nu was held in what was called protective custody until 27 October 1966. In 1969 he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where on 27 August he declared himself the legal prime minister. He formed the Parliamentary Democracy Party, but his armed resistance from the Thai border never numbered more than a few thousand, and he accepted an amnesty and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> on 29 July 1980.</p><p>During the 8888 Uprising he founded the League for Democracy and Peace and in September 1988 again claimed to be the legal prime minister. He was put under house arrest on 29 December 1989 and released on 23 April 1992. He died on 14 February 1995 at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “U Nu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Burmese diplomat U Thant, a close friend of Prime Minister <b>U Nu</b>, served as Secretary-General of the United Nations from 1961 to 1971, the first Asian in that post.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Bandung also brought together <b>U Nu</b>, V. K. Krishna Menon, Ho Chi Minh, Zhou Enlai, Norodom Sihanouk, U Thant and a young Indira Gandhi.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In Burma General Ne Win’s caretaker government, formally invited by Prime Minister <b>U Nu</b> under pressure from the army in 1958, held elections and handed power back in 1960.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Ba Maw governed from Rangoon with a cabinet including Aung San as war minister and <b>Thakin Nu</b> as foreign minister, but the Japanese army controlled the economy and military affairs.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-burma">State of Burma</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wakema, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.6,95.18&amp;year=1964" aria-label="Wakema on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7141 1547 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of U Nu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7591 1817h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7591 1817h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.6,95.18&amp;year=1964">What was here in 1964? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
